51
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.920
independently established hardness status.

52
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.460
The tutorial Hamiltonian Path to

53
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.380
Hamiltonian Cycle mapping adds a

54
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.220
new vertex adjacent to every

55
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.640
original vertex. A Hamiltonian path

56
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:45.920
becomes a cycle by putting

57
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.260
the new vertex before its

58
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:48.880
first endpoint and after its

59
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.780
last. Removing the new vertex

60
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.840
from any resulting Hamiltonian cycle

61
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.820
leaves a path through all

62
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:55.560
old vertices.

63
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.380
For simple cycles requiring at

64
00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.020
least three vertices, handle tiny

65
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.060
source graphs separately. A one-vertex

66
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.860
graph has a Hamiltonian path

67
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.560
but the naive added-vertex graph

68
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.540
has only two vertices and

69
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.640
no simple cycle. Mapping constant-size

70
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.600
exceptional cases to fixed yes

71
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.580
or no target graphs preserves

72
00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.100
a total correct reduction.

73
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.860
Reductions compose. If A reduces

74
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.080
to B and B to

75
00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.560
C, first apply the A-to-B

76
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.420
mapping and then the B-to-C

77
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.860
mapping; output sizes and runtime

78
00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.760
remain polynomial. But A reducing

79
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.340
to B does not generally

80
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.180
imply B reducing to A,

81
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.520
so transitivity must not be

82
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.580
confused with symmetry.

83
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.060
The photo asks what follows

84
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.680
from A reducing to B,

85
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.400
B to C and C

86
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.520
to B. B and C

87
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.120
are equivalent under polynomial reductions

88
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.180
and A also reduces to

89
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.220
C. Nothing in those premises

90
00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.160
requires B to reduce back

91
00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:56.660
to A.

